1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:01,852
Hi, everyone.

2
00:00:02,527 --> 00:00:03,497
I'm Vinay Puri.

3
00:00:04,018 --> 00:00:09,469
Today, I'm-- I want to talk about
building machine learning based compliance

4
00:00:09,469 --> 00:00:16,403
monitoring system using working hour alert
system, WHAS, as a practical case study.

5
00:00:17,063 --> 00:00:20,937
The interesting part of this
problem is actually not building

6
00:00:20,938 --> 00:00:22,223
the machine learning model.

7
00:00:22,668 --> 00:00:28,437
The difficult part is building the system
that can identify unusual behavior at

8
00:00:28,464 --> 00:00:34,518
enterprise scale, explain why something
looks unusual, and still keep a human

9
00:00:34,518 --> 00:00:38,171
investigator in control of the decision.

10
00:00:38,549 --> 00:00:43,412
So I'll walk the architecture, the
data engineering, feature engineering,

11
00:00:43,476 --> 00:00:49,467
anomaly detection approach,
explainability, and some of the lessons

12
00:00:49,474 --> 00:00:53,661
we learned operating this type of
system in real enterprise environment

13
00:00:54,661 --> 00:00:57,304
The, the compliance monitoring problem.

14
00:00:57,859 --> 00:00:59,354
Let me start with the problem.

15
00:00:59,553 --> 00:01:04,455
In a large organization, working
hour information usually doesn't

16
00:01:04,527 --> 00:01:06,411
live in one clean database.

17
00:01:06,780 --> 00:01:12,956
You may have attendance events in
one system and employee schedules

18
00:01:13,005 --> 00:01:17,187
somewhere else, approved leave
in another system, and then HR

19
00:01:17,198 --> 00:01:19,451
information in yet another platform.

20
00:01:20,029 --> 00:01:24,144
So when somebody needs to investigate
a potential compliance issue, a

21
00:01:24,145 --> 00:01:29,639
surprising amount of time can be spent
simply reconstructing what happened.

22
00:01:30,128 --> 00:01:32,628
Traditional monitoring
is usually rule-driven.

23
00:01:32,814 --> 00:01:37,034
For example, did somebody exceed
a specific number of hours?

24
00:01:37,419 --> 00:01:39,904
Did they miss required break?

25
00:01:40,277 --> 00:01:43,960
Those rules are useful, but
they only detect situations we

26
00:01:43,966 --> 00:01:45,791
already know how to describe.

27
00:01:46,593 --> 00:01:50,787
The harder problem is detecting
patterns we didn't explicitly encode

28
00:01:51,787 --> 00:01:56,707
So the next slide I have is the,
the-- that distinction leads

29
00:01:56,712 --> 00:01:59,178
to main architectural idea.

30
00:02:00,345 --> 00:02:06,660
The rule engine asks a very deterministic
question, did condition X happen?

31
00:02:07,713 --> 00:02:11,536
Machine learning allows us to
ask different question, does this

32
00:02:11,548 --> 00:02:16,453
behavior look unusual compared
with what we normally expect?

33
00:02:16,977 --> 00:02:21,963
Suppose an employee-- employee's
individual events are technically valid.

34
00:02:22,092 --> 00:02:25,159
No single event w- violates a policy.

35
00:02:25,489 --> 00:02:31,720
But imagine that over several weeks, we
see unusual start times, abnormal working

36
00:02:31,722 --> 00:02:38,637
durations, repeating gaps, or behavior
that is sp- significantly different from

37
00:02:38,637 --> 00:02:40,963
what a person's historical baseline.

38
00:02:41,674 --> 00:02:45,678
Individually, those events
may mean nothing together.

39
00:02:46,064 --> 00:02:51,070
they may deserve investigation, and that's
where anomaly detection becomes useful.

40
00:02:51,380 --> 00:02:55,176
And I want to emphasize that this
isn't replacing the rules engine.

41
00:02:55,412 --> 00:02:58,358
The, the better design is
combining both approaches.

42
00:02:58,415 --> 00:03:03,531
Rules for policy violations and
machine learning for patterns

43
00:03:03,687 --> 00:03:08,594
that are harder to express
deterministically VAS architecture.

44
00:03:09,248 --> 00:03:14,356
At a high level, VAS can be
thought as a four-stage pipeline.

45
00:03:14,903 --> 00:03:17,384
First, operational data comes in.

46
00:03:17,491 --> 00:03:20,632
Second, we validate and clean the data.

47
00:03:20,732 --> 00:03:24,874
Third, we convert raw events into
features that describe behavior.

48
00:03:25,232 --> 00:03:30,768
And finally, those features are evaluated
to produce a risk or anomaly score.

49
00:03:31,106 --> 00:03:32,644
The separation is important.

50
00:03:33,005 --> 00:03:39,142
I don't want model code trying to
compensate for bad timespan, timestamp

51
00:03:39,154 --> 00:03:45,322
parsing, and I don't want business policy
logic buried inside feature engineering.

52
00:03:45,754 --> 00:03:47,980
Each layer has a clear responsibility.

53
00:03:48,054 --> 00:03:54,626
Also, the output is intentionally a
risk signal rather than a verdict.

54
00:03:54,688 --> 00:03:57,712
The system says this deserves attention.

55
00:03:57,722 --> 00:04:00,057
It does not say this
person did something wrong

56
00:04:01,057 --> 00:04:02,911
Building the data foundation.

57
00:04:04,212 --> 00:04:07,426
This is probably the least
glamorous part of ML engineering,

58
00:04:07,656 --> 00:04:09,409
but often the most important.

59
00:04:10,818 --> 00:04:13,063
Time data is surprisingly messy.

60
00:04:13,476 --> 00:04:17,623
Different systems may represent
timestamps in different time zones.

61
00:04:17,945 --> 00:04:21,733
One system may store scheduled
hours, another actual events,

62
00:04:22,161 --> 00:04:24,479
and another approved exceptions.

63
00:04:24,982 --> 00:04:30,085
You can also have duplicate events,
delayed updates, meeting records,

64
00:04:30,171 --> 00:04:32,762
missing records, or conflicting states.

65
00:04:33,171 --> 00:04:35,490
Of those issues go directly to the model.

66
00:04:36,026 --> 00:04:41,556
The model will happily detect your data
pipeline problems as employee anomalies.

67
00:04:41,938 --> 00:04:48,346
So we use a very deliberate sequence:
ingest the source data, normalize

68
00:04:48,351 --> 00:04:56,599
formats, timestamps, validate relationship
between records, and only then allow

69
00:04:57,144 --> 00:04:59,686
that information into feature generation.

70
00:04:59,758 --> 00:05:04,169
In system like this, improving
data quality can often produce

71
00:05:04,199 --> 00:05:08,479
bigger improvement than moving
to more sophisticated models

72
00:05:09,479 --> 00:05:12,709
Feature engineering for behavior analysis.

73
00:05:13,111 --> 00:05:16,923
Once data is trustworthy,
feature engineering becomes

74
00:05:17,923 --> 00:05:19,107
the heart of the problem.

75
00:05:20,099 --> 00:05:24,054
A raw timestamp isn't particularly
meaningful to a model.

76
00:05:24,388 --> 00:05:28,277
We need to transform events into signals.

77
00:05:28,631 --> 00:05:31,001
One category is duration and timing.

78
00:05:31,083 --> 00:05:35,577
How long somebody worked, when
activity started, how far that

79
00:05:35,577 --> 00:05:37,758
differs from expected schedule.

80
00:05:38,296 --> 00:05:40,024
Another is recurrence.

81
00:05:40,474 --> 00:05:42,948
A single unusual event may be noise.

82
00:05:43,255 --> 00:05:47,344
The same behavior repeatedly
appearing over several days

83
00:05:47,649 --> 00:05:49,184
is much more interesting.

84
00:05:50,462 --> 00:05:52,363
Then we have historical baseline.

85
00:05:52,857 --> 00:05:56,621
Instead of comparing every employee
to one global definition of

86
00:05:56,650 --> 00:06:00,933
normal, we can compare behaviors
against individual's own history.

87
00:06:02,046 --> 00:06:07,022
That matters because normal behaviors
can vary different across job

88
00:06:07,027 --> 00:06:09,364
functions, shifts, and geography.

89
00:06:09,697 --> 00:06:11,974
And finally, we add context.

90
00:06:12,288 --> 00:06:17,147
Approved leave, travel, shift changes,
or operational incidents can completely

91
00:06:17,515 --> 00:06:20,840
change the interpretation of an anomaly.

92
00:06:20,883 --> 00:06:25,354
That's why good feature engineering
is often more valuable than simply

93
00:06:25,357 --> 00:06:27,320
choosing a complicated algorithm.

94
00:06:28,320 --> 00:06:30,542
Choosing the right ML approach.

95
00:06:30,921 --> 00:06:33,006
Now we get into actual machine learning.

96
00:06:33,096 --> 00:06:36,777
A common instinct is to treat
this as a classification problem,

97
00:06:37,376 --> 00:06:39,244
compliant versus non-compliant.

98
00:06:39,547 --> 00:06:41,073
But there is a practical issue.

99
00:06:41,090 --> 00:06:45,965
We have a huge amount of
ordinary behavior, relatively few

100
00:06:45,994 --> 00:06:53,403
confirmed examples of, violations
that creates a very imbalanced

101
00:06:53,403 --> 00:06:55,469
supervised learning problem.

102
00:06:55,790 --> 00:07:01,828
And sometimes we don't even have enough
reliable labels to train a classifier.

103
00:07:02,546 --> 00:07:05,719
Anomaly detection fits
the problem differently.

104
00:07:05,797 --> 00:07:10,044
Instead of trying to learn every
possible bad behavior, we learn

105
00:07:10,063 --> 00:07:16,348
characteristics of expected behavior
and identify significant deviations.

106
00:07:17,304 --> 00:07:19,748
There are multiple
levels of sophistication.

107
00:07:19,967 --> 00:07:24,714
You can begin with interpretable
statistical baselines.

108
00:07:25,079 --> 00:07:28,353
Then clustering can
identify behavioral groups.

109
00:07:28,769 --> 00:07:34,495
Tree-based anomaly detection approaches
can capture more complex interactions

110
00:07:34,499 --> 00:07:41,147
between variables the tree-based anal
pro can capture more complex variables.

111
00:07:41,190 --> 00:07:45,485
The, the representation
learning approaches can go

112
00:07:45,490 --> 00:07:50,007
further, but interpretability
becomes increasingly difficult.

113
00:07:50,257 --> 00:07:54,298
And in compliance system, the most
accurate model isn't automatically

114
00:07:54,308 --> 00:08:00,712
the last model if investigators cannot
understand why a model generated an alert.

115
00:08:00,818 --> 00:08:03,605
Operational trust drops very quickly.

116
00:08:03,658 --> 00:08:10,207
So interpretability is an architectural
requirement, not just a feature.

117
00:08:11,207 --> 00:08:13,433
Anomaly scores are not conclusions.

118
00:08:13,512 --> 00:08:16,978
This is probably the most
important conceptual distinction

119
00:08:17,308 --> 00:08:18,567
in the entire system.

120
00:08:18,924 --> 00:08:21,730
An anomaly score measures unusualness.

121
00:08:22,146 --> 00:08:24,766
It does not measure guilt.

122
00:08:25,470 --> 00:08:30,483
A high score could result from
legitimate schedule change, an operash-

123
00:08:30,528 --> 00:08:35,263
operational emergency, missing data,
an integration failure, or actual

124
00:08:35,280 --> 00:08:37,402
behavior that needs investigation.

125
00:08:38,230 --> 00:08:41,832
So an alert should contain
more than a number.

126
00:08:41,976 --> 00:08:46,887
We want to show observed pattern,
contributing signals, how this compares

127
00:08:46,902 --> 00:08:51,582
with historical behavior, and any
context available to the system.

128
00:08:52,540 --> 00:08:57,627
Then investigator determines that
anomaly usually means the separation

129
00:08:57,629 --> 00:09:02,156
between detection and judgment is
extremely important when machine

130
00:09:02,157 --> 00:09:07,888
learning is being used around
people Designing actionable alerts.

131
00:09:08,247 --> 00:09:11,395
Another operational
problem is alert fatigue.

132
00:09:11,810 --> 00:09:16,320
If you, if your model produces
ten thousand alerts every morning,

133
00:09:16,362 --> 00:09:20,738
technically it may be detecting
anomalies, but operationally,

134
00:09:20,967 --> 00:09:22,843
you have built a useless system.

135
00:09:22,949 --> 00:09:25,237
So alerts need prioritization.

136
00:09:25,252 --> 00:09:30,068
We can consider anomaly strength,
whether the behavior is recurring,

137
00:09:30,083 --> 00:09:33,728
how many independent signals
agree, and whether contextual

138
00:09:33,736 --> 00:09:35,760
information already explains it.

139
00:09:36,103 --> 00:09:38,690
And every alert needs an answer.

140
00:09:39,820 --> 00:09:42,219
Why am I looking at this?

141
00:09:42,359 --> 00:09:45,469
Not just anomaly score point eight seven.

142
00:09:45,843 --> 00:09:47,396
Instead, what changed?

143
00:09:47,438 --> 00:09:49,084
What signals contributed?

144
00:09:49,334 --> 00:09:53,420
And how far was the behavior
from expected baseline?

145
00:09:53,778 --> 00:09:59,695
This turns the model output into
something an investigator can actually use

146
00:10:00,695 --> 00:10:02,786
Human oversight and explainability.

147
00:10:03,420 --> 00:10:08,232
The natural-- this naturally creates
a human-in-the-loop architecture.

148
00:10:08,540 --> 00:10:11,891
Machines are very good at
scanning millions of events and

149
00:10:11,891 --> 00:10:13,632
finding statistical pattern.

150
00:10:14,028 --> 00:10:17,959
Humans are much better at
understanding context and

151
00:10:17,959 --> 00:10:21,526
making consequential judgments.

152
00:10:21,593 --> 00:10:23,389
And there is another benefit.

153
00:10:23,523 --> 00:10:26,090
Every investigation produces a feedback.

154
00:10:26,504 --> 00:10:28,026
Was the alert useful?

155
00:10:28,384 --> 00:10:30,689
Was there a legitimate explanation?

156
00:10:30,762 --> 00:10:32,608
Was there a data quality problem?

157
00:10:32,810 --> 00:10:36,660
The feedback becomes valuable
operational information for

158
00:10:36,662 --> 00:10:38,729
improving thresholds and feature.

159
00:10:39,454 --> 00:10:42,055
But that improvement
should also be governed.

160
00:10:42,314 --> 00:10:47,069
We shouldn't automatically allow
reviewer decision to immediately

161
00:10:47,074 --> 00:10:49,521
retrain a production model.

162
00:10:49,853 --> 00:10:53,840
There should be validation,
versioning, and control deployment

163
00:10:54,840 --> 00:10:57,161
Enterprise integration and reliability.

164
00:10:57,551 --> 00:11:02,903
Once an ML system enters production,
the model is only one small

165
00:11:02,909 --> 00:11:06,185
component of reliability problem.

166
00:11:06,786 --> 00:11:11,387
We need secure ingestion and
controlled access because this

167
00:11:11,389 --> 00:11:13,407
can be sensitive workforce data.

168
00:11:13,839 --> 00:11:18,265
We need observability across entire
pipeline, not just CPU and memory.

169
00:11:18,332 --> 00:11:22,653
We need to monitor data freshness,
feature validity, pipeline

170
00:11:22,665 --> 00:11:27,435
failures, source distributions,
and changes in model behavior.

171
00:11:27,611 --> 00:11:30,359
And graceful degradation
is especially important.

172
00:11:30,805 --> 00:11:36,236
Suppose a scheduling feed fails, but
attendance data continues arriving.

173
00:11:36,440 --> 00:11:39,969
The system should not interpret
missing or scheduled information

174
00:11:40,412 --> 00:11:42,383
as unusual employee behavior.

175
00:11:42,835 --> 00:11:46,603
The safer behavior is to mark
that analysis as incomplete.

176
00:11:47,076 --> 00:11:52,532
Finally, everything needs an audit trail:
data transformations, model versions,

177
00:11:52,985 --> 00:11:55,339
configuration changes, and alert logic.

178
00:11:55,947 --> 00:12:01,560
If somebody asks six months later why an
alert was generated, the system should

179
00:12:01,572 --> 00:12:04,569
be able to reconstruct that decision path

180
00:12:05,569 --> 00:12:08,422
Privacy, security, and
responsible monitoring.

181
00:12:08,835 --> 00:12:12,216
because we are monitoring
workforce behavior, privacy

182
00:12:12,217 --> 00:12:13,853
needs to be part of the design.

183
00:12:14,369 --> 00:12:17,764
A usual principle here
is data minimization.

184
00:12:17,875 --> 00:12:21,075
Just because a feature is
technically available doesn't

185
00:12:21,078 --> 00:12:23,003
mean we should collect it.

186
00:12:23,433 --> 00:12:28,830
Every field should have a defensible
relationship to the monitoring objective.

187
00:12:29,475 --> 00:12:33,230
And governance applies not only
to employee data, it applies

188
00:12:33,233 --> 00:12:35,226
to monitoring platform itself.

189
00:12:35,531 --> 00:12:37,524
Who can change a threshold?

190
00:12:37,537 --> 00:12:38,923
Who can deploy a model?

191
00:12:38,976 --> 00:12:40,510
Who can access an alert?

192
00:12:40,912 --> 00:12:45,179
What transformation produced
is a particular feature?

193
00:12:45,225 --> 00:12:50,700
What control prevent compliance platform
itself from become a compliance risk?

194
00:12:51,700 --> 00:12:54,137
Managing false alerts and model drift.

195
00:12:54,997 --> 00:12:58,979
Prediction model also don't
remain correct forever.

196
00:12:59,104 --> 00:13:04,002
If your threshold is too aggressive,
investigator gets alert fatigue.

197
00:13:04,467 --> 00:13:08,829
If it's too conservative,
important signals disappear.

198
00:13:08,891 --> 00:13:13,808
So thresholds needs to be calibrated
using operational experience.

199
00:13:14,178 --> 00:13:16,081
And there is model drift.

200
00:13:16,172 --> 00:13:17,643
People change behavior.

201
00:13:18,034 --> 00:13:22,275
Organization recognize policy
change, remote work patterns

202
00:13:22,275 --> 00:13:24,872
change, new systems get introduced.

203
00:13:24,899 --> 00:13:28,921
A model trained on historical
behavior may gradually start to

204
00:13:28,968 --> 00:13:31,375
s-stop representing current behavior.

205
00:13:33,002 --> 00:13:36,882
But drift doesn't automatically
mean retrain the model.

206
00:13:37,301 --> 00:13:38,982
First, diagnose the cause.

207
00:13:39,068 --> 00:13:41,342
Did actual workforce behavior change?

208
00:13:41,390 --> 00:13:44,767
Did a source system change
its timestamp format?

209
00:13:45,180 --> 00:13:48,675
Did a new policy legitimately
shift working patterns?

210
00:13:48,973 --> 00:13:52,151
Or is there genuinely a new risk pattern?

211
00:13:52,261 --> 00:13:54,727
Diagnose first, retrain second.

212
00:13:56,655 --> 00:13:58,194
Key engineering lessons.

213
00:13:58,248 --> 00:14:01,553
So if I had to summarize the
engineering lessons, there are four.

214
00:14:01,740 --> 00:14:05,113
First, start with workflow,
not the algorithm.

215
00:14:05,633 --> 00:14:08,404
Understand how
investigation actually work.

216
00:14:08,773 --> 00:14:11,593
Second, prioritize data quality.

217
00:14:11,867 --> 00:14:16,683
Reliable timestamps can create
more value than an exotic model.

218
00:14:17,341 --> 00:14:19,987
Third, transparency drives adoption.

219
00:14:20,088 --> 00:14:24,713
People trust systems when they can
understand the reasoning behind an alert.

220
00:14:24,738 --> 00:14:27,906
And fourth, automate selectively.

221
00:14:28,233 --> 00:14:31,883
Machines should do what machines
are good at, processing enormous

222
00:14:31,885 --> 00:14:33,929
amount of data consistently.

223
00:14:34,209 --> 00:14:38,698
Humans should provide the context,
accountability, judgment required to

224
00:14:38,703 --> 00:14:41,441
consequence- consequential decisions.

225
00:14:41,485 --> 00:14:45,231
The goal isn't maximum automation,
it is effective automation

226
00:14:46,231 --> 00:14:48,308
The future of intelligent compliance.

227
00:14:49,311 --> 00:14:53,229
And although this case study focuses
on working hour compliance, the

228
00:14:53,229 --> 00:14:55,245
architecture is much more general.

229
00:14:55,643 --> 00:14:59,758
The exact same pattern applies to
security event, transaction monitoring,

230
00:14:59,804 --> 00:15:04,866
access behavior, process integrity
monitoring, and many other risk domains.

231
00:15:05,222 --> 00:15:10,288
Observe behavior, evaluate it,
surface anomalies, have humans review

232
00:15:10,288 --> 00:15:17,218
the evidence, use those outcomes to
improve the system, validate change,

233
00:15:17,284 --> 00:15:19,665
deploy them, and continue monitoring.

234
00:15:21,409 --> 00:15:26,685
So I think the future of intelligent
compliance isn't autonomous enforcement.

235
00:15:27,103 --> 00:15:33,160
It's combining large-scale automated
detection with explainable pa-- systems

236
00:15:33,162 --> 00:15:35,225
and accountable human decision-making.

237
00:15:35,269 --> 00:15:40,764
The principle I would leave you with
is appro-- appropriate automation,

238
00:15:40,801 --> 00:15:45,939
not maximum automation This is the
architecture of some of the engineering

239
00:15:45,939 --> 00:15:50,361
lessons behind building ML-based
compliance monitoring at enterprise scale.

240
00:15:50,723 --> 00:15:54,239
If there is one thing I would like
to take away, it's the production

241
00:15:54,290 --> 00:15:56,775
ML is rarely just about a model.

242
00:15:57,175 --> 00:16:01,371
Data quality, explainability,
observability, governments, and

243
00:16:01,374 --> 00:16:05,954
human workflow are what ultimately
determine whether the system works.

244
00:16:05,981 --> 00:16:06,588
Thank you.

245
00:16:06,624 --> 00:16:08,330
I'm Vinay Puri, and I'm happy to take

246
00:16:08,334 --> 00:16:09,746
questions.

